--- /dev/null
+++ b/.crit-loop/round-3/crit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+**First read.** On every viewport the eye lands first on the serif headline, "Run the waiting list for your garden without the spreadsheet". It then drops to the two dark green plots, 3 and 8, in the plot card, and to the "Add your garden" button, which uses the same green. The page reads as a calm, plain product page for people who run community gardens, and it feels trustworthy and unfussy. On tablet and phone the plot card sits below the button and now spans the full width of the content. On widescreen the whole page sits in a 1120 px column with a lot of empty cream below the feature cards.
+
+**What it's for.** It gets the person who runs a community garden's waiting list to add their garden to Plotwise. (This is my assumption from the headline and the button.)
+
+**Verdict.** The fix this round tidied the tablet layout, but it did not change what the page tells a garden secretary. The biggest thing still in the way is the hero picture. It shows two empty plots but not the thing Plotwise does with them, which is to offer the plot to the next person in line.
+
+**Finding status**
+- picture-misses-waiting-list: open. On all four viewports the card still shows only plots 3 and 8 in green, under the caption "Elm Row Gardens, plots 1 to 12". Nothing shows a next grower or an offer.
+- rent-reminder-march: open. On all four viewports the line still reads "Each grower gets one reminder before rent is due in March." It does not say whether March is an example or a fixed date.
+- button-says-nothing-next: open. On all four viewports there is still no line next to "Add your garden".
+- tablet-plot-card-edge: closed. On tablet the plot card now runs from x 64 to x 704, the same as the right edge of the feature card grid below it. Its tiles grew to 95 px wide and nothing in it wraps or breaks.
+
+**New problems.** None.
+
+**What would move it most**
+1. The hero picture does not show the waiting list. This is the plot card in the hero. The viewer sees that plots 3 and 8 are empty, but the page's promise, that the empty plot goes to the next person in line, is left for them to imagine. Make the card show what happens to plot 3 or plot 8, and change the caption to say that. Who is next and what the offer looks like must come from the maker; do not invent them. Taste. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+
+**Smaller things.**
+- Rent reminders card: say whether March is an example or the fixed rent date. Only the maker knows this. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+- "Add your garden" button: add a short line next to it that says what the first step involves and whether it costs anything. Only the maker knows this. Viewports: desktop, phone, tablet, widescreen.
+
+**What's working.**
+- Green means two things, "empty plot" and "the action to take", and nothing else on the page is green. So the plots and the button are the only things that pull the eye after the headline.
+- The type is quiet and readable. The serif headline is at 11.4:1 contrast, body text is at 5.75:1 or better, the measured facts file reports no violations, and the hero paragraph runs at 73 characters per line.
+- On tablet the plot card now uses the full content width, so the hero and the feature grid share one right edge.
